# What answers to `then` hands whatever it likes to the function that it is passed: a promise that is resolved with such an object holds anything, and not the object.
# function later() { return { then(resolve) { resolve(21); } }; }  Bun.sleep(0).then(later).then(behindThen);  fails().catch(later).then(behindCatch);
# function own() { const made = make(); made.then = function (resolve) { resolve(21); }; return made; }  Bun.sleep(0).then(own).then(behindOwn);
# The rows of then | catch settle what the callback returns. If they laid the object into the value of the new promise as it is, whoever reads the value would be typed by the object while a number arrives.
# A promise that was given a `then` of its own is asked like any other object.
# A second thing keeps the three parameters open today: the object escapes, and whatever reaches unseen code opens every promise (somebodyCanBeHandedSomething in AOTLinkReach.cpp). The test holds whichever of the two is narrowed.
# The records are what the front end writes of that text, less the library.
# f1 = make, f2 = later, f3 = fails, f4 = own, f5 = behindThen, f6 = behindCatch, f7 = behindOwn, f8 = then
# RUN: link functions mechanisms=all
# CHECK: function f5
# CHECK-NEXT: parameter 0
# CHECK-NOT: proven=
# CHECK: function f6
# CHECK-NEXT: parameter 0
# CHECK-NOT: proven=
# CHECK: function f7
# CHECK-NEXT: parameter 0
# CHECK-NOT: proven=
# CHECK: function f8

